WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:09.890
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:12.800
So in this lecture, we
have a very brief view

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.620
at the current status
of Higgs boson research.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.600
And I have to tell you
that we could spend

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.360
an entire week discussing this.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:22.940
I just give you the
high-level overview, maybe

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.700
the 30,000 feet kind
of overview of what

00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.650
we know about the Higgs boson.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:30.560
On the Canvas page,
you'll find a reference

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:32.330
to a summary report,
which gives you

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:35.700
a little bit more information
than I give you here.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.120
But I do think that there's
a few things to highlight.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:41.590
And those are the ones which
I'm going to talk about.

00:00:41.590 --> 00:00:45.710
The first part is that we
have discovered the Higgs

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:50.120
boson in decays to photons and
also in decays to the Z bosons.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:53.240
Where the Z boson itself
decays into a pair of leptons,

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:54.770
electrons and muons.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:57.170
And the detectors
we have available--

00:00:57.170 --> 00:01:00.440
and here is an example
for ATLAS and for CMS--

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:04.550
they are very good in measuring
with precision the energy

00:01:04.550 --> 00:01:09.530
or momenta of photons,
electrons, and muons.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:13.030
So this allows us
to then reconstruct

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:17.500
the mass of the Higgs boson
as it goes through the decay.

00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:19.780
And you can see
this here, this is

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:24.510
125 GeV reconstructed
two-photon final state.

00:01:24.510 --> 00:01:26.890
So we have Higgs--

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:29.290
two photons from ATLAS.

00:01:29.290 --> 00:01:31.090
You see that there
is a slight bump

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.460
over an enormous background.

00:01:33.460 --> 00:01:36.490
So those are other
sources of diphoton events

00:01:36.490 --> 00:01:39.240
which are produced
in a hadron collider.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:41.740
But when you subtract those two
spectra which is shown here,

00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:45.400
data minus background, you see
this beautiful peak of Higgs

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:47.140
to gamma gamma events.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:51.430
Similarly, Higgs to ZZ--

00:01:51.430 --> 00:01:53.890
and I put a little star here,
because one of the Z bosons

00:01:53.890 --> 00:01:55.450
has to be off-shell.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:02:00.820
Z boson mass is 91 GeV, the
mass of the Higgs boson 125 GeV.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:04.590
So 91 plus 91 is 128.

00:02:04.590 --> 00:02:09.100
So one has to be a
little off its mass peak.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:13.230
And then we look at decays
into e plus e minus,

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:16.500
and mu plus mu minus,
and combination of those,

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.830
for example for muon events and
for electron events as well.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:23.550
Also shown here, and again,
you have this beautiful peak

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:27.660
consistent with the Higgs
boson at a mass of 125 GeV.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.700
And again, you have
other processes

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:32.070
which contribute to
this final state,

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:36.180
namely the one where you
have the four leptons coming

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:41.070
from the Z boson itself and/or
from a pair of Z bosons,

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:42.300
as is shown here.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.490
There's also
processes which mimic

00:02:44.490 --> 00:02:46.740
the leptons in the detector.

00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:48.480
Those have to be
evaluated as well.

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:53.150
And they're typically shown
here in this plot here in green.

00:02:53.150 --> 00:02:53.650
OK.

00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:57.400
So as you can imagine, we can
measure the cross-section very

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:01.480
precisely, because we can
identify those particles well.

00:03:01.480 --> 00:03:02.860
But we can also
measure the mass.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:04.300
And this is shown here.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.940
This is a summary of
measurements from ATLAS and CMS

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:10.240
using those to final
states, one with two photons

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.340
or the one with four leptons.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:15.700
And you see that measurements
are generally in agreement,

00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:20.860
and they can be combined to
this measurement of 125 GeV.

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:24.080
And the best, the combined
value is shown here.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.850
So this is a
precision measurement.

00:03:25.850 --> 00:03:28.010
And since the Higgs
boson mass now

00:03:28.010 --> 00:03:30.410
is the only unknown
parameter of the Higgs

00:03:30.410 --> 00:03:34.100
in the standard
model, we know and can

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:36.960
check all other properties.

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:40.250
And one is the coupling strength
to the bosons, which we just

00:03:40.250 --> 00:03:42.290
looked at already, or
the coupling strength

00:03:42.290 --> 00:03:43.550
to the fermions.

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:46.490
So this is two
examples, one that's

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:49.910
showing Higgs to
tau tau events--

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:51.740
tau plus tau minus.

00:03:51.740 --> 00:03:54.710
And you see again, there's a
lot of background processes

00:03:54.710 --> 00:03:57.350
which mimic-- have the
same signature, the signal.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:59.810
But we can find,
when you subtract

00:03:59.810 --> 00:04:02.420
the background from the
data, the nexus of event,

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:05.060
again at 125 GeV.

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:08.000
So taus themselves decay, and
they decay into neutrinos,

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:10.580
which cannot be measured
at these detectors.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:12.290
Therefore, the
mass reconstruction

00:04:12.290 --> 00:04:15.710
is much harder than in the
final stage we discussed before.

00:04:15.710 --> 00:04:17.800
That is easier in
this final state,

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:22.570
where we have a Higgs into
two muons, mu plus mu minus.

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:26.110
The issue here is that
the weight is very small.

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:28.040
The number of Higgs
boson decaying to mu mu

00:04:28.040 --> 00:04:29.170
is very small.

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:31.130
But there's also a large
amount of background.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:33.267
So again, very similar to
the Higgs to gamma gamma

00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:34.600
final state we looked at before.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:36.550
We have a huge
amount of background.

00:04:36.550 --> 00:04:38.440
And here by eye, you
don't even see the bump.

00:04:38.440 --> 00:04:41.200
You see the bump
a little bit when

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:42.910
you do data minus background.

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:44.400
It's a fraction.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:46.180
You see this small
axis here, which

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:48.590
is consistent with 3
standard deviations.

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:50.870
So the likelihood
of the background

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:53.710
to fluctuate without the
presence of the Higgs signal

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.440
is about 3 standard deviations.

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:56.940
All right.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:58.410
This information
then can be used

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:00.940
to extract information
about the couplings itself.

00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:02.770
And this is a
beautiful plot here,

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:05.910
which shows the Higgs
coupling on one axis,

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.220
either to the
fermions or the boson,

00:05:08.220 --> 00:05:10.840
versus the mass of the particle.

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:14.250
And so you find your favorite
particle, the top, the W, Z,

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.290
the bottom, the
tau, and the muon.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:20.310
Those are the ones where we can
actually measure the coupling.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:22.320
And you see whether or
not this is in agreement

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.500
with the standard model, which
is this blue dotted line here.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.540
And you see it is.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:31.590
And that plot here tells
me that the Higgs mechanism

00:05:31.590 --> 00:05:36.990
is responsible for the mass
generation of this particle.

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.720
So it might be that there
is a mechanism which

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:42.690
is very similar to the Higgs
boson which results in the very

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:46.320
same observations
in nature, but it's

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.270
not quite what we have
in the standard model.

00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:50.010
In order to make
those statements,

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:54.260
one has to improve the size
of the error bars here,

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:56.750
or the statistical significance
or the significance

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:58.370
of the measurements.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:06:00.080
That is part of our program.

00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:02.760
That's what we're trying to do.

00:06:02.760 --> 00:06:03.260
All right.

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:06.320
In summary-- again, this is
a very high-level summary--

00:06:06.320 --> 00:06:09.260
we measured the mass, we
measured the spin and CP

00:06:09.260 --> 00:06:12.270
of Higgs boson, we
measured the coupling

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:16.320
to the Z, the W, the top, the
bottom, the tau, and the muon.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.350
We have not been able to measure
the coupling to lighter quark

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:20.790
masses--

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:24.390
strange, charm, up, and down.

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:27.570
We have not been able to measure
the coupling to the electron.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:29.320
That would be spectacular.

00:06:29.320 --> 00:06:31.532
And we have not measured
the coupling to itself.

00:06:31.532 --> 00:06:33.240
So in the standard
model, the Higgs boson

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:34.590
can couple to itself.

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:40.080
So we have diagrams which look
like this, Higgs, Higgs Higgs.

00:06:40.080 --> 00:06:43.090
And we have not been
able to measure this.

00:06:43.090 --> 00:06:44.900
This is not free in
the standard model,

00:06:44.900 --> 00:06:48.580
and this would be closing
the argument that the Higgs

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:50.800
boson requires mass--

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.080
or the Higgs boson
is the particle

00:06:53.080 --> 00:06:55.480
predicted in the standard
model giving mass

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:58.870
to the W and Z boson.

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:01.300
If you want, you can take
the strength of the coupling

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:02.170
here as this lambda.

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:04.610
This is the lambda
term in our potential.

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:07.060
So that's what we are trying
to measure in the future.

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:09.880
But there's more open questions.

00:07:09.880 --> 00:07:12.520
Maybe there's more
than one Higgs boson.

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:16.000
The Higgs-- adding one
duplet to the standard model

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.480
with this potential is
one possible solution

00:07:19.480 --> 00:07:21.520
to the problem of
generating mass.

00:07:21.520 --> 00:07:25.390
But you could have very well
added two, or m, or triplets,

00:07:25.390 --> 00:07:27.290
or more complicated things.

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:31.360
And so the question is, are
those maybe realized in nature

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:31.890
or not?

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:37.850
Will more precision
tell us something

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:39.410
about the Higgs boson?

00:07:39.410 --> 00:07:42.530
Are there decays of the Higgs
bosons of nonstandard model

00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:47.010
particles, for example the Higgs
boson decaying into those guys

00:07:47.010 --> 00:07:51.010
here, which could be
evidence for that matter?

00:07:51.010 --> 00:07:51.640
We have looked.

00:07:51.640 --> 00:07:52.570
We have not seen this.

00:07:52.570 --> 00:07:54.980
But more precision might
give us a different answer.

00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:58.090
That's basically the status
of the summary of where

00:07:58.090 --> 00:07:59.320
we are with the Higgs boson.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:01.390
Again, much more can
be said about that.

00:08:01.390 --> 00:08:03.640
And you will see this
every now and then

00:08:03.640 --> 00:08:07.260
in seminars at MIT
or other places.

